Affine auxiliary Fourier projection memory and timing, 2026-09-09

Baseline: wavebit/_core/conforming/affine_cell.py at 67cff19.
Candidate: zero-mode weak assembly, then x Fourier projection batches of 16.
The unchanged dependencies and all other production code are shared.
Host: macOS arm64, Apple M4, 16 GiB physical RAM, Apple Accelerate.
OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 and OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 were set; these do not establish
Accelerate's actual thread count. No other numerical task from this session
overlapped this paired comparison.
Installed versions: numpy=2.5.1, scipy=1.18.0, jax=0.11.0

Reproduction: load the baseline affine module under a distinct module name
within wavebit._core.conforming, and instantiate one old and one new model.
Use AffineCellGapModel(12, x_subdivisions=2, rows=4, body_degree=4,
gap_degree=6, flux_basis='local', edge_fraction=1e-5). This is coefficient
cap 24, with 3887 auxiliary unknowns. Call coefficients(17.,
{'seams': [31.], 'values': [4., 9.]}, 350.,
basis=700*np.array([[1., .45], [0., .7]])).

Warm each implementation once. Four alternating pairs run before/after,
after/before, before/after, after/before. Collect garbage before each call,
then time only coefficients() with time.monotonic. Compare all nine arrays
against the first baseline warmup; every recorded difference is zero.
After timing, collect garbage and measure one call per implementation with
tracemalloc, keeping only its returned arrays alive. Model construction,
imports and native linear-algebra workspaces are excluded. Traced allocation
is not process RSS, and this does not time matrix closure or Maxwell solving.
The benchmark retains a small reference coefficient array, constructed before
tracing. No JAX memory or performance conclusion follows from this NumPy run.

